# SOLID Architecture

Apply SOLID to every module — plain functions, classes, hooks, and React
components alike. Prefer small, focused units that compose.

## S — Single Responsibility

Each module/function/component does one thing.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: fetches, formats, and renders
export function CharacterCard({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const [data, setData] = useState<Character | null>(null)
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/characters/${id}`)
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((c) => setData({ ...c, name: c.name.toUpperCase() }))
  }, [id])
  return <div>{data?.name}</div>
}

// ✅ GOOD: split fetching, formatting, and rendering
const useCharacter = (id: string) => useQuery(['character', id], () => api.getCharacter(id))
const formatName = (c: Character) => c.name.toUpperCase()

export function CharacterCard({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const { data } = useCharacter(id)
  return <div>{data ? formatName(data) : null}</div>
}
```

## O — Open/Closed

Extend behavior via props, slots, or composition — not by editing existing
components for every new variant.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: branches per variant inside the component
<Button kind="primary" /> // adds another `if (kind === 'ghost')` next time

// ✅ GOOD: accept render slots / className, compose specialized wrappers
<Button className="bg-primary">Save</Button>
export const GhostButton = (p: ButtonProps) => <Button {...p} className="bg-transparent" />
```

## L — Liskov Substitution

A specialized component must remain usable wherever its base contract is
expected. Don't silently drop or repurpose props.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: IconButton ignores `onClick` when `disabled` is set externally
// ✅ GOOD: IconButton<ButtonProps> forwards every prop and only adds an icon
export const IconButton = ({ icon, ...rest }: ButtonProps & { icon: ReactNode }) => (
  <Button {...rest}>{icon}</Button>
)
```

## I — Interface Segregation

Components and functions accept only the props/args they actually use.
Don't pass whole entities when a field will do.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: forces callers to have a full User just to render a name
function Greeting({ user }: { user: User }) { return <p>Hi {user.name}</p> }

// ✅ GOOD
function Greeting({ name }: { name: string }) { return <p>Hi {name}</p> }
```

## D — Dependency Inversion

Depend on abstractions, not concretions. Inject side-effectful collaborators
(API clients, stores, timers) so units can be tested and swapped.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: component imports the concrete API client directly
import { apiClient } from '@/api/client'

// ✅ GOOD: receive the dependency (or a hook that wraps it)
type SendMessage = (text: string) => Promise<void>
export function ChatInput({ sendMessage }: { sendMessage: SendMessage }) { /* … */ }
```

## React-specific guidance

- Split a component when it has more than one reason to re-render or change.
- Move data fetching into hooks (`useX`); keep components rendering-only.
- Keep prop surfaces narrow; lift shared logic into hooks or utilities
  rather than growing one mega-component.
